# CARBonAra — Apptainer definition.
#
# Context-aware protein sequence design: given a backbone, it predicts which amino acids
# fit each position, conditioned on everything else in the structure — other chains,
# nucleic acids, ligands, ions, even waters if you leave them in. Unlike most inverse
# folding models it reads no sequence information at all, only geometry and element
# identity, which is what lets it design around a non-protein partner.
#
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR=cu12 carbonara-cu12.sif carbonara.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR=cu13 carbonara-cu13.sif carbonara.def
#   apptainer build --fakeroot --build-arg CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR=cpu  carbonara-cpu.sif  carbonara.def
#
# Pick by the TARGET machine's driver, not by its GPU: the driver caps the CUDA major
# version, minor versions are forward compatible and major versions are not. A cu13 image
# on a 560-series driver aborts at CUDA init with "The NVIDIA driver on your system is too
# old". See the top-level README for the three gates in full.
#
# The model is 6 MB and a single design is seconds, so `cpu` is a real option — worth it
# for a handful of structures, or anywhere you would rather not negotiate a GPU driver.
# Sampling many sequences per scaffold is where a GPU starts to pay.
#
# Two things about packaging this one:
#
#   1. THE WEIGHTS ARE INSIDE THE PACKAGE. setup.py ships model/save/<variant>/model.pt as
#      package_data, and the CARBonAra class resolves it relative to its own __file__. So
#      there is nothing to download, nothing to bind-mount, and no HF cache to warm. The
#      previous version of this definition ran a model-loading line labelled "pre-download
#      model weights"; it downloaded nothing and was only ever a smoke test. That job now
#      belongs to %test, which does it properly on a real structure.
#   2. NUMPY IS NOT PINNED, DELIBERATELY. Sibling models from this group need `numpy<2`
#      because they predate the numpy 2 removals. CARBonAra does not: the whole of
#      carbonara.py and src/ was checked for the removed aliases (np.float, np.bool,
#      np.int, in1d, row_stack, alltrue) and uses none of them. If a future rebuild fails
#      inside preprocessing with an AttributeError on a numpy attribute, that check is what
#      to repeat before adding a pin.

BootStrap: docker
From: python:3.11.14-slim-bookworm

%arguments
    CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR=cu12

%labels
    Maintainer  Benedikt Singer
    Tool        CARBonAra
    Upstream    https://github.com/LBM-EPFL/CARBonAra
    Commit      f3b942f94bf5b9cbf2b973c9c0581c73f180576b
    Accelerator {{ CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR }}
    License     CC BY-NC-SA 4.0

%environment
    export CARBONARA_HOME=/opt/CARBonAra
    export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

    # The SIF is read-only and $HOME may be unwritable or shared between concurrent jobs.
    # Point everything that insists on writing at node-local /tmp.
    export XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/carbonara-cache
    export MPLCONFIGDIR=/tmp/carbonara-cache/mpl
    export CUDA_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/carbonara-cache/nv
    export TORCH_HOME=/tmp/carbonara-cache/torch

%post
    set -eux
    export DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive
    export PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1

    CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR={{ CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR }}
    CARBONARA_COMMIT=f3b942f94bf5b9cbf2b973c9c0581c73f180576b

    apt-get update
    apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ca-certificates git
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

    python -m pip install --no-cache-dir "uv>=0.13.4"

    git clone https://github.com/LBM-EPFL/CARBonAra.git /opt/CARBonAra
    git -C /opt/CARBonAra checkout --quiet "${CARBONARA_COMMIT}"
    rm -rf /opt/CARBonAra/.git

    # Torch backend follows the accelerator. `--torch-backend` makes uv resolve the wheel
    # index itself, which is what keeps the CUDA libraries inside the wheels and lets this
    # build start from a plain python base image rather than an nvidia/cuda one.
    case "${CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR}" in
        cu13) TORCH_ARGS="--torch-backend=cu130" ;;
        cu12) TORCH_ARGS="--torch-backend=cu126" ;;
        cpu)  TORCH_ARGS="--torch-backend=cpu" ;;
        *) echo "CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR must be cu12, cu13 or cpu (got '${CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR}')" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac

    # Install from the clone. setup.py reads requirements.txt, so the dependency list stays
    # upstream's; only torch's wheel index is ours.
    uv pip install --system --no-cache ${TORCH_ARGS} /opt/CARBonAra

    # The examples are kept: %test uses 2oob.pdb, and it is the fastest way for a user to
    # check the image does something before pointing it at their own structure.
    test -f /opt/CARBonAra/examples/2oob.pdb

    # Assert the weights really travelled with the package. package_data is quiet when it
    # misses a pattern, so a broken install looks identical to a good one until the first
    # run fails with FileNotFoundError on model.pt.
    export CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR_CHECK="${CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR}"
    python - <<'PYEOF'
import os
import pathlib

import carbonara
import torch

root = pathlib.Path(carbonara.__file__).parent
variant = root / "model" / "save" / "s_v6_4_2022-09-16_11-51"
weights = variant / "model.pt"
cdf = variant / "s_v6_4_2022-09-16_11-51_cdf.csv"

for path in (weights, cdf, variant / "config.py", variant / "model.py"):
    assert path.is_file(), "missing from the installed package: {}".format(path)
# A stopped download or a half-copied file still has the right name; the real checkpoint is
# 6.4 MB, so anything under a megabyte is a truncation, not a smaller model.
assert weights.stat().st_size > 1_000_000, "model.pt is {} bytes".format(weights.stat().st_size)

accel = os.environ["CARBONARA_ACCELERATOR_CHECK"]
archs = torch.cuda.get_arch_list()
print("torch", torch.__version__, "accelerator", accel)
print("arch list:", archs)
if accel == "cpu":
    assert not archs, "cpu build should carry no CUDA archs, got {}".format(archs)
else:
    # sm_90 is Hopper and is present in both cu126 and cu130 wheels; its absence means the
    # torch backend selection did not take effect.
    assert any(a.startswith("sm_90") for a in archs), archs
    if accel == "cu13":
        assert any(a.startswith("sm_100") for a in archs), \
            "cu13 build has no Blackwell kernels: {}".format(archs)
print("packaged weights OK:", weights.stat().st_size, "bytes")
PYEOF

    # Shrink the image: pip's own build tooling is not needed at run time.
    python -m pip uninstall -y uv || true
    find / -xdev -name '__pycache__' -type d -prune -exec rm -rf {} + || true

%test
    set -eu
    # A real design on a real structure, on the CPU, so this test runs identically on a
    # build node with no GPU. It exercises the whole path: PDB parsing, feature encoding,
    # topology extraction, a forward pass, confidence mapping and FASTA output.
    #
    # What this test CANNOT tell you: whether the image runs on your cluster. It never
    # touches the driver and never initialises a GPU. Both of those fail at run time, in a
    # real job, and they fail with their own distinct errors — see the README.
    OUT="$(mktemp -d)"
    carbonara --device cpu --num_sequences 2 --sampling_method sampled \
        /opt/CARBonAra/examples/2oob.pdb "$OUT"
    test -s "$OUT/2oob_0.fasta"
    test -s "$OUT/2oob_pssm.csv"
    test -s "$OUT/2oob_scaffold.pdb"
    head -c 200 "$OUT/2oob_0.fasta"; echo
    rm -rf "$OUT"
    echo "CARBONARA_TEST_OK"

%runscript
    exec carbonara "$@"

%help
    CARBonAra — context-aware protein sequence design (inverse folding).

      apptainer exec --nv -B "$PWD":/data carbonara-cu12.sif \
        carbonara --device cuda --num_sequences 10 --imprint_ratio 0.5 \
          /data/scaffold.pdb /data/out

    Positional arguments are the input PDB and an output DIRECTORY (not a file). For each
    run you get <name>_scaffold.pdb (the structure as the model saw it), <name>_pssm.csv
    (per-position amino-acid probabilities) and <name>_<k>.fasta, one per sampled sequence
    with its score in the header. Chains are joined by ':' inside a single FASTA record.

    Options worth knowing:

      --num_sequences N       sequences to sample (default 1)
      --imprint_ratio R       how much of the sampled sequence is fed back in during
                              iterative sampling, 0-1 (default 0.5). Lower is more diverse,
                              higher is more self-consistent.
      --sampling_method       'sampled' (default) or 'max'. This chooses only how the
                              FIRST, imprint-free prediction is turned into a sequence:
                              'sampled' draws from the per-position confidence, 'max' takes
                              the argmax. NEITHER IS REPRODUCIBLE -- see below.
      --known_chains A,B      keep these chains' sequences fixed and design the rest
      --known_positions ...   comma-separated residue INDICES (0-based, over the whole
                              structure) to hold fixed
      --unknown_positions ... the inverse: design only these indices
      --ignored_amino_acids C,M   exclude residue types from sampling (cysteine-free
                              designs, methionine-free for expression, and so on)
      --ignore_hetatm         drop HETATM records. Default is to KEEP them, which is the
                              point of this model: ligands, ions and nucleic acids are
                              context it designs against.
      --ignore_water          drop waters (default keeps them)
      --device cuda|cpu       default cuda. With no usable GPU torch falls back to CPU
                              silently, so a job that is mysteriously slow is worth
                              checking here first.

    REPRODUCIBILITY: THERE IS NO SEED, AND `max` IS NOT DETERMINISTIC.

    Each sampling iteration masks the imprint at random --
    `mm = np.random.rand(n) <= (1 - imprint_ratio)` in imprint_sampling -- and nothing
    seeds numpy. So --sampling_method max gives you N DIFFERENT sequences for
    --num_sequences N, and re-running the same command gives different answers again.
    Measured on examples/2oob.pdb at the default imprint_ratio=0.5: two independent
    comparisons agreed at 107/117 (91.5%) and 109/117 (93.2%).

    Two settings are deterministic, because they make that random mask irrelevant:

      --imprint_ratio 1.0   nothing is masked, so the imprint is exactly the sampled
                            sequence (most self-consistent)
      --imprint_ratio 0.0   everything is masked, so every iteration reduces to the
                            imprint-free prediction (and all N sequences come out equal)

    Anywhere in between, treat the output as a sample and not as a result you can quote by
    its sequence. If you need a reproducible pipeline, record the FASTAs you got.

    This image installs the s_v6_4 variant, which is the one upstream ships and the one the
    paper reports. The repository also contains s_v6_5, but setup.py does not package it,
    so it is not in site-packages here.

    Nothing is downloaded at run time: weights ride inside the installed package, so the
    image works with no network.

